<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahle Selassie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sahle Selassie was ruler of Shewa in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1813 to 1847, the first of his line to take the title negus, king. He expanded Shewa into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> lands, combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian kingship with alliances with Muslim and Oromo neighbours, and received British and French missions. He was the grandfather of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie.jpg" alt="Sahle Selassie" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Sahla_Sellase_colour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sahle Selassie was a younger son of Wossen Seged. When his father was murdered, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> rebels in Marra Biete kept his elder brother Bakure from reaching the capital at Qundi, and the teenage Sahle Selassie left the monastery at Sela Dingay, where he was a student, and was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> and Meridazmach of Shewa, probably with the support of his mother Zenebework's kinsmen in Menz. Bakure was imprisoned at Gonchu with his other brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He won over the Abichu Oromo and defeated the Tulama Oromo in the early 1820s, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a>, founded fortified villages such as Angolalla, and pushed the frontier into Bulga, Karayu and Arsi and south to the Gurage. A contemporary account of one raid near Entótto tells of houses burned, old men and children killed and women and children carried off as captives. In the north he kept a buffer of Yejju and Wollo Oromo rulers between Shewa and lords such as Ras Ali II.</p><p>A famine in 1829, cholera from 1830 and the rebellion of his general Medoko, put down in 1834 or 1835, threatened Shewa; in the drought and famine that followed he opened the royal storehouses to the needy. His attempt to place loyal men over the monasteries brought him into conflict with the Ichege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a>, and in 1845 the new Abuna, Salama III, excommunicated him. He signed treaties of friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> on 7 June 1841 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 16 November 1841, raised his stock of firearms to 500 by 1840 and double that by 1842, and welcomed foreign craftsmen, though he expelled two Protestant missionaries in 1842.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He limited executions to treason, sacrilege and murder, required his own approval for them, and urged victims' relatives to accept blood money rather than take a murderer's life. He reformed taxation, kept large stores of salt against the closing of the roads, and around 1840 his cash revenue is estimated at between 80,000 and 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> thalers; the historian Abir called Shewa an archaic example of a welfare state. In failing health in his last years, he died in Debre Birhan in 1847.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sahle Selassie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sahle Selassie</b> (r. 1813 to 1847), who took the title negus, built a wealthy state and received British and French missions.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shewa, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9,39&amp;year=1828" aria-label="Shewa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5627 1828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sahle Selassie"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6077 2098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6077 2098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9,39&amp;year=1828">What was here in 1828? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
